WEBVTT

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:00.204
to some very early work of mine,

00:03:00.204 --> 00:03:03.090
and this was the kind of stuff I was doing in the '60s:

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:05.277
very direct manipulation,

00:03:05.277 --> 00:03:07.932
very influenced as I studied architecture

00:03:07.932 --> 00:03:10.019
by the architect Moshe Safdie,

00:03:10.019 --> 00:03:12.781
and you can see that we even built robotic things

00:03:12.781 --> 00:03:15.537
that could build habitat-like structures.

00:03:15.537 --> 00:03:17.224
And this for me was

00:03:17.224 --> 00:03:19.140
not yet the Media Lab,

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:21.957
but was the beginning of what I'll call

00:03:21.957 --> 00:03:23.476
sensory computing,

00:03:23.476 --> 00:03:25.348
and I pick fingers

00:03:25.348 --> 00:03:29.550
partly because everybody thought it was ridiculous.

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:31.705
Papers were published